 "literal": "Bearing transgression always, that forgetting even than that is good.",
 "translation": "It is always good to endure a transgression, but forgetting it is even better.",
 "modern": "பிறர் செய்யும் குற்றத்தை எப்போதும் பொறுத்துக்கொள்வது நல்லது. ஆனால் அக்குற்றத்தை மனதிலிருந்து முற்றிலும் மறந்துவிடுவது பொறுத்துக்கொள்வதையும் விட மிகவும் சிறந்தது.",
 "explanation": "This couplet compares two noble responses to someone else's wrongdoing. The word 'iṟappu' can mean death or passing, but here it takes the sense of a transgression or an act that goes beyond acceptable limits. While 'poṟuttal' (bearing or forgiving) such an act is always a virtuous practice, 'maṟattal' (completely forgetting the offense) is praised as even better. This is because merely forgiving might still leave a trace of the wrong in the mind, whereas forgetting removes the memory entirely, ensuring absolute inner peace.",
